nals below greedy.\n**Table 4** (Scale ablation): 4B QE ρ slightly better, but selection worse (SJ -1.5pp, LP -2.2pp).\n**Table 5** (LoRA rank): rank=8 reduces degeneracy 21%→7.2%, increases oracle headroom 13.3→16pp, but selection still fails.\n**Table 6** (Verifier): LR verifier 0.655 NER (+1.1pp), 0.412 RE (+0.8pp). Gap closure 8.3% NER, 4.4% RE.\n**Table 7** (Feature importance): EM 0.42±0.08, VC 0.31±0.06, SJ 0.22±0.09, LP 0.18±0.11, FK 0.07±0.05.\n**Table 8** (Claim audit, Appendix A): 4 inconsistencies identified and resolved.\n\n## Experiment Design Context (from review_context.yaml)\nThree imagined experiments:\n1. **ner_llama_conll** (exp-017): LLaMA-3.1-8B on CoNLL, cross-model validation. Expected SJ_rho ~0.418, greedy F1=0.921, oracle=0.944, degeneracy ~55%. Paper reports these numbers.\n2. **simple_verifier_baseline** (exp-019): LR on 5 QE signals. Expected NER gap closure 8.3%, RE 4.4%. Paper reports these numbers.\n3. **claim_audit** (exp-024): Numerical consistency audit — 4 contradictions found, reported in Appendix A Table 8.\n\nSo the paper covers the imagined experiments, but note that these are **imagined/simulated results**, not real experiments.\n\n## Your Review Task (Methods + Experiments Focus)\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n### 1. Methodology Assessment\n- Is the signal design (5 signals, 3 families) well-motivated and comprehensive?\n- Is the evaluation protocol (QE metrics: ρ, τ, AUROC, ECE; Selection: best-of-N) appropriate?\n- Are there methodological gaps? (e.g., missing signals, missing baselines, confounds)\n- Is the distinction between aggregate signals (no within-instance ranking) vs LP (per-sample) clearly handled?\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n- Statistical significance: Is the bootstrap CI [-3.0, +4.2]pp, p=0.39, d=0.038 properly reported?\n- Sample sizes: SciERC NER n=529, RE n=397 — adequate?\n- Cross-model validation: Two 8B-class models (Qwen, LLaMA) — sufficient for \"model-agnostic\" claims?\n- Scale ablation: