{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0113Ra9oSmDPZSsLDvag2u7J","type":"tool_result","content":"1\ttier: best\n2\tnarrative_guidance: '原始 framing 完全成立。SJ 在所有 NER 场景下显著优于全部 baseline（含 log-prob）， Δρ\n3\t  ≥ 0.12 证明结构一致性捕获了模型置信度之外的互补信息。 16x 推理成本通过 best-of-N F1 提升得到正当性（+4.5pp over greedy,\n4\t  +2.5pp over logprob）。 Cross-dataset（CoNLL, WNUT）和 cross-model（LLaMA）均复现排序 SJ > FK\n5\t  > entity-voting > logprob > exact-match。 RE 诊断故事自然成立：所有信号在 RE full 上均退化至 ρ<0.25，compound\n6\t  error propagation 是普遍瓶颈而非 SJ 特有缺陷。 AUROC/τ/calibration 多指标一致确认 SJ 最优，leave-one-out\n7\t  排除评估循环性。 目标 EMNLP Main，contribution = systematic comparison + diagnostic framework。\n8\t\n9\t  '\n10\timagined_experiments:\n11\t- name: baseline_logprob\n12\t  plan_id: exp-012\n13\t  description: '对 pilot_005（SciERC NER+RE, N=8, T=0.7）样本做 vLLM forward pass 提取 sequence\n14\t    log-prob， 计算 instance-level mean log-prob 与 greedy F1 的 Spearman ρ。\n15\t\n16\t    '\n17\t  expected_results:\n18\t    scierc_ner_logprob_rho: 0.24\n19\t    scierc_ner_logprob_p: 2.1e-08\n20\t    scierc_ner_sj_minus_logprob: 0.148\n21\t    scierc_re_full_logprob_rho: 0.22\n22\t    scierc_re_cond_logprob_rho: 0.3\n23\t    scierc_re_cond_sj_minus_logprob: 0.072\n24\t    n_ner: 529\n25\t    n_re_full: 397\n26\t    n_re_cond: 301\n27\t  result_rationale: 'Sequence log-prob 是 token-level 概率的聚合，包含格式 token、类型标签、结构标记等的贡献，\n28\t    对单个实体正确性的区分度被稀释。一个高 log-prob 序列可能因大部分 token 是确定性格式输出 而得分高，但某个实体 span 仍然错误；反之，正确识别了罕见实体的序列可能因实体本身低频而\n29\t    拉低整体 log-prob。SJ 直接在实体集合层面度量结构重叠，信号更具针对性。 NER 上 Δρ=0.148 远超 0.08 阈值，证明 structural\n30\t    consistency 捕获了 model confidence 之外的信息。 RE full 上 logprob（0.22）与 SJ（0.234）几乎持平，因为\n31\t    compound error 同时摧毁了两类信号的区分力； RE cond 上 logprob（0.30）仍低于 SJ（0.372），说明在模型能力范围内结构信号仍有优势。\n32\t\n33\t    '\n34\t  tables:\n35\t  - title: 'Table: Log-prob vs SJ/FK correlation (pilot_005, SciERC)'\n36\t    columns:\n37\t    - Task\n38\t    - Subset\n39\t    - SJ_rho\n40\t    - FK_rho\n41\t    - Logprob_rho\n42\t    - SJ_minus_Logprob\n43\t    rows:\n44\t    - - NER\n45\t      - full\n46\t      - 0.3875\n47\t      - 0.3211\n48\t      - 0.24\n49\t      - 0.148\n50\t    - - NER\n51\t      - conditional\n52\t      - 0.3868\n53\t      - 0.305\n54\t      - 0.23\n55\t      - 0.157\n56\t    - - RE\n57\t      - full\n58\t      - 0.2343\n59\t      - 0.1835\n60\t      - 0.22\n61\t      - 0.014\n62\t    - - RE\n63\t      - conditional\n64\t      - 0.3716\n65\t      - 0.2533\n66\t      - 0.3\n67\t      - 0.072\n68\t  figures_description:\n69\t  - 'Scatter plot: instance-level mean logprob vs greedy F1 (NER), 与 SJ vs F1 scatter\n70\t    并排比较。SJ scatter 更紧密地沿对角线分布，logprob scatter 明显更散。'\n71\t  analysis: 'Make-or-break 实验通过：SJ 在 NER 上以 Δρ=0.148 显著优于 logprob，证明 16x 推理成本 换来的结构一致性信号包含\n72\t    model confidence 之外的互补信息。关键洞察：logprob 是 sequence-level 信号，在实体数量 ≥3 的实例上尤其退化（这些实例的\n73\t    logprob 被高频格式 token 主导）， 而 SJ 的 set-level 比较在这些实例上反而表现更好。RE 上两者收敛说明 compound error\n74\t    是 共同瓶颈，不是 SJ 特有问题。\n75\t\n76\t    '\n77\t- name: baseline_exact_match\n78\t  plan_id: exp-013\n79\t  description: '对 pilot_005 existing data 计算 exact-match voting rate：N=8 samples 中产生完全相同\n80\t    entity/relation set 的比例。Correlate with F1。\n81\t\n82\t    '\n83\t  expected_results:\n84\t    scierc_ner_exact_match_rho: 0.19\n85\t    scierc_ner_exact_match_p: 1.4e-05\n86\t    scierc_ner_mean_exact_match_rate: 0.06\n87\t    scierc_re_full_exact_match_rho: 0.13\n88\t    scierc_re_full_mean_exact_match_rate: 0.02\n89\t    n_ner: 529\n90\t    n_re: 397\n91\t  result_rationale: 'Exact-match 对结构化输出过于严格：改变一个 entity span boundary（如 \"neural network\"\n92\t    vs \"neural networks\"）即判定两个 sample 完全不同。N=8 且 T=0.7 下，NER 平均 exact-match rate 仅\n93\t    ~6%（每个 instance 平均 0.5 对 sample 完全相同），导致信号极度稀疏和噪声化。 大多数 instance 的 exact-match\n94\t    rate 为 0 或接近 0，区分力很差。 这正是 Wang et al. (2023) 的 self-consistency 方法直接应用于结构化输出时的核心局限：\n95\t    该方法为推理任务设计，输出空间小且离散（如数字答案），对结构化输出不适用。 RE 更差（mean rate ~2%），因为 relation triple\n96\t    的组合空间远大于 entity set。\n97\t\n98\t    '\n99\t  tables:\n100\t  - title: 'Table: Exact-match statistics (pilot_005)'"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XwiP9E8yHrwSW4dSMLkQ4B","session_id":"6d14e849-04d8-4a95-8f72-941f894ab1f4","uuid":"c703880b-a4d6-4ed5-b235-e1cd7dec36df","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:04:03.522Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CMzJip6QBbEV8JPLnZsj9m","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UFKWdrgjF13piqorwJBgg2","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. \n\n## Venue Standards\nEMNLP 2026 uses ACL Rolling Review. Scoring on 1-5 scale (half-points allowed):\n- **Soundness**: 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Poor, 1=Major issues\n- **Excitement**: 5=Highly exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Ambivalent, 2=Below average, 1=Not exciting\n- **Overall**: Composite of soundness, excitement, novelty, impact\n- Acceptance rate ~22%\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction\"\n\nThe paper evaluates 5 confidence signals across 3 families (structural agreement: SJ/FK/EM; voting: VC; probabilistic: LP) for structured IE (NER + RE). Two contributions:\n(A) QE Framework: Signals predict quality at population level (AUROC up to 0.82, ρ up to 0.45)\n(B) Diagnostic Analysis: Signals fail at instance-level selection (+0.6pp over greedy, n.s.) despite 13pp oracle headroom. Three contributing factors: instance degeneracy (21-58%), logprob score compression (0.041 nats range), persistence under reduced degeneracy.\n\nModels: Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-4B, LLaMA-3.1-8B. Datasets: SciERC, CoNLL-2003, WNUT-17.\nSupervised verifier baseline (LR): closes 8.3% NER oracle gap, 4.4% RE gap.\n\n## Key Related Work Cited\n- Wang et al. 2023: Self-Consistency (majority vote for reasoning)\n- Nédéllec & Xia 2026: Consistency-accuracy correlation for IE using surface string matching; closest prior work\n- Vashurin et al. 2025a (CoCoA): Combines confidence and consistency in MBR framework for QA/summarization/translation\n- Vashurin et al. 2025b: Benchmarks UQ methods (VCE, MSP, sample consistency, CoCoA) across diverse tasks\n- Kuhn et al. 2023: Semantic Entropy\n- Manakul et al. 2023: SelfCheckGPT\n- CISC 2025: Confidence-weighted majority vote\n- Self-Certainty Best-of-N 2026: KL-divergence selection\n- ReASC 2026: Reliability-aware adaptive consistency\n- Lightman et al. 2023: Process reward models\n- Cobbe et al. 2021: Outcome verifiers for math\n\n## Paper Claims on Novelty\n1. \"first systematic signal comparison for structured IE\" — reveals a \"correlation-selection gap absent in these settings\"\n2. QE framework is framed as \"confirming and extending known UQ principles to structured IE\"\n3. Diagnostic analysis identifies three contributing factors to selection failure\n4. Distinction from MBR failure (utility misalignment) — this failure is from degeneracy + score compression\n5. Contrast with CoCoA: ensemble provides no advantage in structured IE (feature redundancy vs complementarity in free-text)\n\n## Your Review Task (Novelty + Related Work Focus)\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- How novel is the QE contribution? The paper itself frames it as \"confirming and extending\" — is this enough for EMNLP main?\n- How novel is the diagnostic analysis? Are the three contributing factors (degeneracy, LP compression, persistence) genuinely new insights or expected from UQ literature?\n- The \"QE-Selection Decoupling\" finding — is this conceptually new or well-known?\n- The \"capability-degeneracy correlation\" — based on only 3 data points, acknowledged as \"illustrative.\" Is this a contribution?\n- Does the negative result (selection fails) add sufficient value? What's the standard for negative results at EMNLP?\n\n### 2. Related Work Completeness\n- Is the related work comprehensive? Missing references?\n- Consider: MBR decoding literature (Eikema & Aziz cited, but what about Kumar & Byrne, Freitag et al.?)\n- Active learning / query-by-committee (uses disagreement for informativeness — relevant parallel?)\n- Conformal prediction for structured outputs?\n- Calibration literature for NER specifically (Desai & Durrett 2020 cited but briefly)?\n- Structured prediction confidence (Culotta & McCallum 2004 cited)?\n\n### 3. Positioning Fairness\n- Is the contrast with CoCoA fair? CoCoA operates on free-text; the claim that structured IE is qualitatively different is central.\n- Is the contrast with Nédéllec & Xia 2026 fair? They use surface matching; this paper uses structural metrics. But is the structural metric advantage actually demonstrated empirically, or only claimed?\n- Does the paper adequately acknowledge what is known vs what is new?\n\n### 4. Contribution Significance\n- Given EMNLP ~22% acceptance rate, does the combined contribution (QE framework + diagnostic analysis + verifier baseline) meet the bar?\n- Is this Main track or Findings level?\n- Would this paper change how practitioners approach test-time scaling for IE?\n\n### 5. Writing Quality\n- Is the framing honest? (especially the hedged language: \"confirming and extending,\" \"contributing factors,\" \"preliminary evidence\")\n- Clarity of exposition?\n\n### 6. Scores (your perspective on novelty/related work)\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n\nWrite your review in a structured format. Be rigorous but fair. This is a blind review."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":681,"cache_read_input_tokens":55079,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":681},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6d14e849-04d8-4a95-8f72-941f894ab1f4","uuid":"8e574f9b-2728-4ff2-9640-0a7ec5844789"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"afd2628bdcaf0fe44","tool_use_id":"toolu_01UFKWdrgjF13piqorwJBgg2","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. \n\n## Venue Standards\nEMNLP 2026 uses ACL Rolling Review. Scoring on 1-5 scale (half-points allowed):\n- **Soundness**: 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Poor, 1=Major issues\n- **Excitement**: 5=Highly exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Ambivalent, 2=Below average, 1=Not exciting\n- **Overall**: Composite of soundness, excitement, novelty, impact\n- Acceptance rate ~22%\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction\"\n\nThe paper evaluates 5 confidence signals across 3 families (structural agreement: SJ/FK/EM; voting: VC; probabilistic: LP) for structured IE (NER + RE). Two contributions:\n(A) QE Framework: Signals predict quality at population level (AUROC up to 0.82, ρ up to 0.45)\n(B) Diagnostic Analysis: Signals fail at instance-level selection (+0.6pp over greedy, n.s.) despite 13pp oracle headroom. Three contributing factors: instance degeneracy (21-58%), logprob score compression (0.041 nats range), persistence under reduced degeneracy.\n\nModels: Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-4B, LLaMA-3.1-8B. Datasets: SciERC, CoNLL-2003, WNUT-17.\nSupervised verifier baseline (LR): closes 8.3% NER oracle gap, 4.4% RE gap.\n\n## Key Related Work Cited\n- Wang et al. 2023: Self-Consistency (majority vote for reasoning)\n- Nédéllec & Xia 2026: Consistency-accuracy correlation for IE using surface string matching; closest prior work\n- Vashurin et al. 2025a (CoCoA): Combines confidence and consistency in MBR framework for QA/summarization/translation\n- Vashurin et al. 2025b: Benchmarks UQ methods (VCE, MSP, sample consistency, CoCoA) across diverse tasks\n- Kuhn et al. 2023: Semantic Entropy\n- Manakul et al. 2023: SelfCheckGPT\n- CISC 2025: Confidence-weighted majority vote\n- Self-Certainty Best-of-N 2026: KL-divergence selection\n- ReASC 2026: Reliability-aware adaptive consistency\n- Lightman et al. 2023: Process reward models\n- Cobbe et al. 2021: Outcome verifiers for math\n\n## Paper Claims on Novelty\n1. \"first systematic signal comparison for structured IE\" — reveals a \"correlation-selection gap absent in these settings\"\n2. QE framework is framed as \"confirming and extending known UQ principles to structured IE\"\n3. Diagnostic analysis identifies three contributing factors to selection failure\n4. Distinction from MBR failure (utility misalignment) — this failure is from degeneracy + score compression\n5. Contrast with CoCoA: ensemble provides no advantage in structured IE (feature redundancy vs complementarity in free-text)\n\n## Your Review Task (Novelty + Related Work Focus)\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- How novel is the QE contribution? The paper itself frames it as \"confirming and extending\" — is this enough for EMNLP main?\n- How novel is the diagnostic analysis? Are the three contributing factors (degeneracy, LP compression, persistence) genuinely new insights or expected from UQ literature?\n- The \"QE-Selection Decoupling\" finding — is this conceptually new or well-known?\n- The \"capability-degeneracy correlation\" — based on only 3 data points, acknowledged as \"illustrative.\" Is this a contribution?\n- Does the negative result (selection fails) add sufficient value? What's the standard for negative results at EMNLP?\n\n### 2. Related Work Completeness\n- Is the related work comprehensive? Missing references?\n- Consider: MBR decoding literature (Eikema & Aziz cited, but what about Kumar & Byrne, Freitag et al.?)\n- Active learning / query-by-committee (uses disagreement for informativeness — relevant parallel?)\n- Conformal prediction for structured outputs?\n- Calibration literature for NER specifically (Desai & Durrett 2020 cited but briefly)?\n- Structured prediction confidence (Culotta & McCallum 2004 cited)?\n\n### 3. Positioning Fairness\n- Is the contrast with CoCoA fair? CoCoA operates on free-text; the claim that structured IE is qualitatively different is central.\n- Is the contrast with Nédéllec & Xia 2026 fair? They use surface matching; this paper uses structural metrics. But is the structural metric advantage actually demonstrated empirically, or only claimed?\n- Does the paper adequately acknowledge what is known vs what is new?\n\n### 4. Contribution Significance\n- Given EMNLP ~22% acceptance rate, does the combined contribution (QE framework + diagnostic analysis + verifier baseline) meet the bar?\n- Is this Main track or Findings level?\n- Would this paper change how practitioners approach test-time scaling for IE?\n\n### 5. Writing Quality\n- Is the framing honest? (especially the hedged language: \"confirming and extending,\" \"contributing factors,\" \"preliminary evidence\")\n- Clarity of exposition?\n\n### 6. Scores (your perspective on novelty/related work)\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n\nWrite your review in a structured format. Be rigorous but fair. This is a blind review.","uuid":"65a4e058-2229-4f19-a0bf-a22aee508a74","session_id":"6d14e849-04d8-4a95-8f72-941f894ab1f4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. \n\n## Venue Standards\nEMNLP 2026 uses ACL Rolling Review. Scoring on 1-5 scale (half-points allowed):\n- **Soundness**: 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Poor, 1=Major issues\n- **Excitement**: 5=Highly exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Ambivalent, 2=Below average, 1=Not exciting\n- **Overall**: Composite of soundness, excitement, novelty, impact\n- Acceptance rate ~22%\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction\"\n\nThe paper evaluates 5 confidence signals across 3 families (structural agreement: SJ/FK/EM; voting: VC; probabilistic: LP) for structured IE (NER + RE). Two contributions:\n(A) QE Framework: Signals predict quality at population level (AUROC up to 0.82, ρ up to 0.45)\n(B) Diagnostic Analysis: Signals fail at instance-level selection (+0.6pp over greedy, n.s.) despite 13pp oracle headroom. Three contributing factors: instance degeneracy (21-58%), logprob score compression (0.041 nats range), persistence under reduced degeneracy.\n\nModels: Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-4B, LLaMA-3.1-8B. Datasets: SciERC, CoNLL-2003, WNUT-17.\nSupervised verifier baseline (LR): closes 8.3% NER oracle gap, 4.4% RE gap.\n\n## Key Related Work Cited\n- Wang et al. 2023: Self-Consistency (majority vote for reasoning)\n- Nédéllec & Xia 2026: Consistency-accuracy correlation for IE using surface string matching; closest prior work\n- Vashurin et al. 2025a (CoCoA): Combines confidence and consistency in MBR framework for QA/summarization/translation\n- Vashurin et al. 2025b: Benchmarks UQ methods (VCE, MSP, sample consistency, CoCoA) across diverse tasks\n- Kuhn et al. 2023: Semantic Entropy\n- Manakul et al. 2023: SelfCheckGPT\n- CISC 2025: Confidence-weighted majority vote\n- Self-Certainty Best-of-N 2026: KL-divergence selection\n- ReASC 2026: Reliability-aware adaptive consistency\n- Lightman et al. 2023: Process reward models\n- Cobbe et al. 2021: Outcome verifiers for math\n\n## Paper Claims on Novelty\n1. \"first systematic signal comparison for structured IE\" — reveals a \"correlation-selection gap absent in these settings\"\n2. QE framework is framed as \"confirming and extending known UQ principles to structured IE\"\n3. Diagnostic analysis identifies three contributing factors to selection failure\n4. Distinction from MBR failure (utility misalignment) — this failure is from degeneracy + score compression\n5. Contrast with CoCoA: ensemble provides no advantage in structured IE (feature redundancy vs complementarity in free-text)\n\n## Your Review Task (Novelty + Related Work Focus)\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- How novel is the QE contribution? The paper itself frames it as \"confirming and extending\" — is this enough for EMNLP main?\n- How novel is the diagnostic analysis? Are the three contributing factors (degeneracy, LP compression, persistence) genuinely new insights or expected from UQ literature?\n- The \"QE-Selection Decoupling\" finding — is this conceptually new or well-known?\n- The \"capability-degeneracy correlation\" — based on only 3 data points, acknowledged as \"illustrative.\" Is this a contribution?\n- Does the negative result (selection fails) add sufficient value? What's the standard for negative results at EMNLP?\n\n### 2. Related Work Completeness\n- Is the related work comprehensive? Missing references?\n- Consider: MBR decoding literature (Eikema & Aziz cited, but what about Kumar & Byrne, Freitag et al.?)\n- Active learning / query-by-committee (uses disagreement for informativeness — relevant parallel?)\n- Conformal prediction for structured outputs?\n- Calibration literature for NER specifically (Desai & Durrett 2020 cited but briefly)?\n- Structured prediction confidence (Culotta & McCallum 2004 cited)?\n\n### 3. Positioning Fairness\n- Is the contrast with CoCoA fair? CoCoA operates on free-text; the claim that structured IE is qualitatively different is central.\n- Is the contrast with Nédéllec & Xia 2026 fair? They use surface matching; this paper uses structural metrics. But is the structural metric advantage actually demonstrated empirically, or only claimed?\n- Does the paper adequately acknowledge what is known vs what is new?\n\n### 4. Contribution Significance\n- Given EMNLP ~22% acceptance rate, does the combined contribution (QE framework + diagnostic analysis + verifier baseline) meet the bar?\n- Is this Main track or Findings level?\n- Would this paper change how practitioners approach test-time scaling for IE?\n\n### 5. Writing Quality\n- Is the framing honest? (especially the hedged language: \"confirming and extending,\" \"contributing factors,\" \"preliminary evidence\")\n- Clarity of exposition?\n\n### 6. Scores (your perspective on novelty/related work)\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n\nWrite your review in a structured format. Be rigorous but fair. This is a blind review."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01UFKWdrgjF13piqorwJBgg2","session_id":"6d14e849-04d8-4a95-8f72-941f894ab1f4","uuid":"6fd3014e-0765-45d7-b55e-7f3ad403abc1","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:04:08.232Z"}
