{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VTXSzRgnJKMtBgcWL8P9pS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01E9osSyHSnoNs4ivUouFFys","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_1/projects/struct_self_consist_ie/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T113255/best/paper.tex","limit":300,"offset":0},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":330,"cache_read_input_tokens":68113,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":330,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":67,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XwiP9E8yHrwSW4dSMLkQ4B","session_id":"6d14e849-04d8-4a95-8f72-941f894ab1f4","uuid":"e1823f64-dc2a-4cf0-bd4a-b7d650a1699f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E9osSyHSnoNs4ivUouFFys","type":"tool_result","content":"0\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n1\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n2\t\\usepackage{times}\n3\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n4\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n5\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n6\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n7\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n8\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n9\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n10\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n11\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n12\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n13\t\\usepackage{pgfplots}\n14\t\\pgfplotsset{compat=1.18}\n15\t\n16\t\\title{Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction}\n17\t\n18\t\\author{Anonymous}\n19\t\n20\t\\begin{document}\n21\t\\maketitle\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{abstract}\n24\tTest-time scaling via multi-sample generation improves LLM reasoning tasks, but its effectiveness for structured information extraction (IE) remains unclear. We present a dual contribution: (1)~a quality estimation (QE) framework showing that five confidence signals across four families reliably predict structured IE output quality (AUROC up to 0.82, Spearman $\\rho$ up to 0.44), enabling practical selective prediction; and (2)~a diagnostic analysis revealing why these same signals universally fail at best-of-N selection. We identify a multi-causal failure mechanism: instance degeneracy (21--58\\% of instances produce identical outputs across N samples), logprob score compression (within-instance range 0.041 nats), and insufficient signal discriminability even when degeneracy is reduced. A trained verifier (logistic regression on 5 signals) closes 36\\% of the oracle gap for NER (+4.8pp over greedy), confirming that signals carry exploitable information but unsupervised aggregation is the bottleneck. Experiments span 3 model configurations (Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-4B, LLaMA-3.1-8B), 2 IE tasks (NER, RE), and 3 datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, WNUT-17), establishing that the correlation--selection gap is model-agnostic and task-general.\n25\t\\end{abstract}\n26\t\n27\t%=============================================================================\n28\t\\section{Introduction}\n29\t%=============================================================================\n30\t\n31\tTest-time scaling through multi-sample generation and selection has emerged as a powerful paradigm for improving LLM performance on reasoning tasks~\\cite{wang2023selfconsistency,brown2024large,snell2024scaling}. Self-consistency---sampling multiple chain-of-thought paths and selecting via majority vote---yields substantial gains for discrete-answer problems. However, structured information extraction (IE) outputs---entity sets, relation triples, event structures---resist naive majority voting due to their combinatorial output space.\n32\t\n33\tA natural question arises: can confidence signals derived from multi-sample agreement serve as quality estimators or selectors for structured IE? We decouple these two functions and show they are governed by fundamentally different factors.\n34\t\n35\t\\paragraph{Contribution A: QE Framework.} We systematically evaluate five confidence signals across four families---structural similarity (set Jaccard, fuzzy match), probabilistic (logprob), voting-based (entity voting confidence), and exact match---for structured IE quality estimation. Signals achieve AUROC up to 0.82 and $\\rho$ up to 0.44, demonstrating practical value for selective prediction and error flagging. Signal effectiveness is task-dependent: structural signals dominate for NER while logprob is strongest for RE.\n36\t\n37\t\\paragraph{Contribution B: Diagnostic Analysis.} We diagnose why reliable quality estimators fail as selectors. The failure is multi-causal: (1)~four of five signals are instance-level aggregates that cannot rank candidates by construction; (2)~instance degeneracy eliminates selection headroom for 21--58\\% of instances; (3)~logprob compression (within-instance range 0.041 nats) neutralizes the only per-sample signal; (4)~reducing degeneracy via lower LoRA rank is necessary but not sufficient---signals still cannot exploit increased headroom. A trained verifier closes 36\\% of the NER oracle gap, proving the gap is closable with supervision.\n38\t\n39\tThis dual contribution---positive QE utility paired with mechanistic selection failure diagnosis---provides both practical tools and theoretical understanding for practitioners considering test-time scaling for structured IE.\n40\t\n41\t%=============================================================================\n42\t\\section{Related Work}\n43\t%=============================================================================\n44\t\n45\t\\paragraph{Self-Consistency and Test-Time Scaling.}\n46\tWang et al.~\\cite{wang2023selfconsistency} show that sampling diverse reasoning paths and selecting via majority vote improves accuracy on arithmetic and commonsense tasks. Subsequent work extends this to confidence-weighted voting~\\cite{cisc2025}, reward-free selection via KL-divergence~\\cite{selfcertainty2026}, reliability-aware adaptive consistency~\\cite{reasc2026}, and atomic sub-claim consistency for long-form text~\\cite{atomicsc2024}. All operate on discrete answers or decomposable claims; none address structured outputs with set-valued semantics.\n47\t\n48\t\\paragraph{Consistency for Structured IE.}\n49\tN\\'{e}dellec and Xia~\\cite{nedellec2026} study consistency--accuracy correlation for hard-prompted LLMs on biomedical NER/RE, finding positive correlation but using surface-level string matching that ``overlooks semantically consistent outputs.'' Our structural Jaccard metric directly addresses this limitation. They do not study best-of-N selection or multi-signal comparison.\n50\t\n51\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty Quantification for LLMs.}\n52\tSemantic entropy~\\cite{kuhn2023semantic}, SelfCheckGPT~\\cite{manakul2023selfcheckgpt}, and conformal prediction~\\cite{fisch2021,angelopoulos2021} provide uncertainty estimates for generation. Surveys~\\cite{uqsurvey2025,calibsurvey2025} systematize the landscape. Token-level NER uncertainty has been studied via MC-dropout~\\cite{shelmanov2021} and ensemble methods~\\cite{lakshminarayanan2017}. We complement this line by evaluating sample-level structural consistency signals specifically for structured IE quality estimation.\n53\t\n54\t\\paragraph{MBR Decoding and Verifiers.}\n55\tMinimum Bayes Risk decoding~\\cite{eikema2022} selects candidates minimizing expected loss under a utility function. CoCoA~\\cite{cocoa2025} combines confidence and consistency for MBR-style selection in QA and summarization. Trained verifiers~\\cite{cobbe2021,lightman2023} learn to score candidate solutions. Our work differs: we show that for structured IE, utility \\emph{is} aligned (within-instance $\\rho$=0.524) but headroom is eliminated by degeneracy---a qualitatively distinct failure from utility misalignment.\n56\t\n57\t\\paragraph{Selective Prediction.}\n58\tGeifman and El-Yaniv~\\cite{geifman2017} formalize selective prediction with guaranteed risk. Kuleshov and Liang~\\cite{kuleshov2015} address calibration for structured outputs. Our QE framework provides the confidence scores needed for selective prediction in structured IE, and our diagnostic analysis explains why selection (choosing \\emph{which} output to return) fails even when estimation (knowing \\emph{how good} an output is) succeeds.\n59\t\n60\t%=============================================================================\n61\t\\section{Method}\n62\t%=============================================================================\n63\t\n64\t\\subsection{Problem Setup}\n65\t\n66\tGiven a structured IE model $M$ and input $x$, we generate $N$ samples $\\{y_1, \\ldots, y_N\\}$ at temperature $T{=}1.0$. Each sample $y_i$ is a structured output (entity set or relation set). We evaluate two tasks:\n67\t\\begin{enumerate}\n68\t    \\item \\textbf{Quality Estimation (QE)}: Predict the F1 score of each sample or the greedy output without access to ground truth.\n69\t    \\item \\textbf{Selection}: Choose the sample $y^* = \\arg\\max_i s(y_i)$ that maximizes F1, using signal $s$.\n70\t\\end{enumerate}\n71\t\n72\t\\subsection{Confidence Signals}\n73\t\n74\tWe evaluate five signals across four families:\n75\t\n76\t\\paragraph{Set Jaccard (SJ).} For sample $y_i$ with entity set $E_i$, compute pairwise Jaccard similarity against all other samples and average:\n77\t\\begin{equation}\n78\t\\text{SJ}(y_i) = \\frac{1}{N{-}1} \\sum_{j \\neq i} \\frac{|E_i \\cap E_j|}{|E_i \\cup E_j|}\n79\t\\end{equation}\n80\tEntity matching uses type-grouped Hungarian alignment with $\\max(|g_a|, |g_b|)$ denominator normalization.\n81\t\n82\t\\paragraph{Fuzzy Match (FK).} Replaces exact entity matching with token-level F1 overlap, capturing partial agreement on entity boundaries.\n83\t\n84\t\\paragraph{Logprob (LP).} Mean token-level log-probability of the generated sequence: $\\text{LP}(y_i) = \\frac{1}{|y_i|} \\sum_t \\log p(y_{i,t} | y_{i,<t}, x)$.\n85\t\n86\t\\paragraph{Exact Match (EM).} Fraction of other samples producing an identical entity set: $\\text{EM}(y_i) = \\frac{1}{N{-}1} \\sum_{j \\neq i} \\mathbb{1}[E_i = E_j]$.\n87\t\n88\t\\paragraph{Voting Confidence (VC).} Entity-level voting across $N$ samples; confidence for sample $y_i$ is the mean vote proportion of its constituent entities across the sample pool.\n89\t\n90\t\\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}\n91\t\n92\t\\paragraph{QE Metrics.} Spearman $\\rho$ and Kendall $\\tau$ (rank correlation with F1), AUROC (binary: above/below median F1), and Expected Calibration Error (ECE).\n93\t\n94\t\\paragraph{Selection Metrics.} F1 of the selected sample vs.\\ greedy (T=0) baseline. Statistical significance via bootstrap (1000 iterations) with Cohen's $d$ for effect size.\n95\t\n96\t\\paragraph{Oracle.} Upper bound: $\\max_i \\text{F1}(y_i)$ across N samples.\n97\t\n98\t%=============================================================================\n99\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n100\t%=============================================================================\n101\t\n102\t\\subsection{Models}\n103\tWe use three model configurations: Qwen3-8B (LoRA fine-tuned, rank=32), Qwen3-4B (same recipe), and LLaMA-3.1-8B (LoRA fine-tuned). All models are instruction-tuned for JSON-structured IE output.\n104\t\n105\t\\subsection{Datasets}\n106\t\\begin{itemize}\n107\t    \\item \\textbf{SciERC}~\\cite{luan2018multi}: Scientific NER (6 types) and RE (7 types). 529 NER / 397 RE test instances.\n108\t    \\item \\textbf{CoNLL-2003}~\\cite{tjong2003introduction}: News NER (4 types). 2,756 test instances.\n109\t    \\item \\textbf{WNUT-17}: Emerging/rare entity NER for out-of-distribution analysis.\n110\t\\end{itemize}\n111\t\n112\t\\subsection{Inference Configuration}\n113\t$N{=}8$ samples per instance, temperature $T{=}1.0$, max 1024 tokens. All results report 3-seed means where applicable.\n114\t\n115\t%=============================================================================\n116\t\\section{Results}\n117\t%=============================================================================\n118\t\n119\t\\subsection{Quality Estimation Effectiveness}\n120\t\n121\tTable~\\ref{tab:qe_main} reports QE metrics on SciERC (Qwen3-8B). All structural signals significantly outperform logprob for NER ($\\rho$: 0.295--0.379 vs.\\ 0.205). For RE, binary dominance (43--45\\% zero-F1 instances) shifts the optimal signal family: LP full $\\rho$=0.266 but conditional $\\rho$=0.011---LP's RE advantage is entirely a binary classification effect.\n122\t\n123\t\\begin{table}[t]\n124\t\\centering\n125\t\\small\n126\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n127\t\\toprule\n128\tSignal & $\\rho$ & $\\tau$ & AUROC & ECE \\\\\n129\t\\midrule\n130\t\\multicolumn{5}{c}{\\textit{NER (n=529)}} \\\\\n131\t\\midrule\n132\tVC & 0.379 & 0.312 & 0.684 & 0.381 \\\\\n133\tSJ & 0.360 & 0.298 & 0.692 & 0.453 \\\\\n134\tEM & 0.295 & 0.271 & 0.749 & 0.353 \\\\\n135\tFK & 0.267 & 0.221 & 0.629 & 0.451 \\\\\n136\tLP & 0.205 & 0.158 & 0.578 & 0.706 \\\\\n137\t\\midrule\n138\t\\multicolumn{5}{c}{\\textit{RE (n=397)}} \\\\\n139\t\\midrule\n140\tEM & --- & --- & 0.706 & --- \\\\\n141\tVC & --- & --- & 0.694 & --- \\\\\n142\tSJ & --- & --- & 0.670 & --- \\\\\n143\tFK & --- & --- & 0.609 & --- \\\\\n144\tLP & --- & --- & 0.601 & --- \\\\\n145\t\\bottomrule\n146\t\\end{tabular}\n147\t\\caption{Quality estimation metrics on SciERC (Qwen3-8B, N=8, T=1.0). NER: structural signals (VC, SJ) dominate in rank correlation; EM achieves highest AUROC. RE: all signals discriminate above chance.}\n148\t\\label{tab:qe_main}\n149\t\\end{table}\n150\t\n151\t\\subsection{Cross-Model Validation}\n152\t\\label{sec:cross_model}\n153\t\n154\tTo establish model-agnostic QE effectiveness, we evaluate LLaMA-3.1-8B on CoNLL-2003 NER alongside Qwen3-8B and Qwen3-4B (Table~\\ref{tab:cross_model}).\n155\t\n156\t\\begin{table}[t]\n157\t\\centering\n158\t\\small\n159\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n160\t\\toprule\n161\tModel & SJ $\\rho$ & EM AUROC & Greedy F1 & Degen.\\% \\\\\n162\t\\midrule\n163\tQwen3-8B & 0.436 & 0.816 & 0.912 & 58 \\\\\n164\tLLaMA-8B & 0.431 & 0.804 & 0.924 & 56 \\\\\n165\tQwen3-4B & 0.422 & 0.776 & 0.923 & 55 \\\\\n166\t\\bottomrule\n167\t\\end{tabular}\n168\t\\caption{Cross-model QE comparison on CoNLL-2003 NER (N=8, T=1.0). Signal rankings and correlation magnitudes are consistent across architectures ($\\Delta\\rho < 0.015$).}\n169\t\\label{tab:cross_model}\n170\t\\end{table}\n171\t\n172\tThe cross-model $\\rho$ delta between LLaMA-8B and Qwen3-8B is $-0.005$ (CI crosses zero), confirming that QE signal effectiveness is a property of the task, not the model architecture. All three configurations exhibit high degeneracy rates (55--58\\%) on this well-solved benchmark (greedy F1 $>$ 0.91), consistent with the capability--degeneracy tradeoff.\n173\t\n174\tTable~\\ref{tab:llama_qe} presents the full signal comparison for LLaMA-8B on CoNLL.\n175\t\n176\t\\begin{table}[t]\n177\t\\centering\n178\t\\small\n179\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n180\t\\toprule\n181\tSignal & $\\rho$ & $\\tau$ & AUROC & ECE \\\\\n182\t\\midrule\n183\tEM & 0.435 & 0.387 & 0.804 & 0.110 \\\\\n184\tSJ & 0.431 & 0.382 & 0.792 & 0.115 \\\\\n185\tVC & 0.426 & 0.378 & 0.789 & 0.095 \\\\\n186\tFK & 0.418 & 0.371 & 0.774 & 0.107 \\\\\n187\tLP & 0.307 & 0.241 & 0.761 & 0.131 \\\\\n188\t\\bottomrule\n189\t\\end{tabular}\n190\t\\caption{LLaMA-3.1-8B QE metrics on CoNLL-2003 NER (N=8, T=1.0). Signal ranking (structural $>$ LP) is preserved cross-architecture. EM achieves highest AUROC (0.804); LP $\\rho$ substantially lower than structural signals.}\n191\t\\label{tab:llama_qe}\n192\t\\end{table}\n193\t\n194\t\\subsection{Selection Failure Analysis}\n195\t\n196\tDespite strong QE correlations, no signal significantly outperforms greedy decoding at selection on SciERC:\n197\t\\begin{itemize}\n198\t    \\item NER: LP 0.650, SJ 0.645, Greedy 0.644 ($\\Delta$=+0.6pp, $p$=0.39, $d$=0.038)\n199\t    \\item RE: Greedy 0.404 $>$ all signals\n200\t\\end{itemize}\n201\t\n202\tOn CoNLL (LLaMA-8B), selection F1 matches greedy within 0.1pp: SJ 0.925, LP 0.924, Greedy 0.924. With 56\\% of instances degenerate (identical F1 across all 8 samples), selection has effectively no headroom to exploit.\n203\t\n204\t\\paragraph{Multi-Causal Diagnosis.} The selection failure is not attributable to a single cause:\n205\t\\begin{enumerate}\n206\t    \\item \\textbf{Instance degeneracy}: 21\\% (SciERC) to 58\\% (CoNLL) of instances produce identical F1 across all $N$ samples.\n207\t    \\item \\textbf{LP compression}: Within-instance logprob range is 0.041 nats (median), insufficient for reliable ranking.\n208\t    \\item \\textbf{Discriminability gap}: LoRA rank=8 reduces degeneracy to 7.2\\% and increases oracle headroom (+2.7pp), yet all selection methods \\emph{still} fail to beat greedy---low degeneracy is necessary but not sufficient.\n209\t\\end{enumerate}\n210\t\n211\t\\subsection{Trained Verifier Closes the Gap}\n212\t\\label{sec:verifier}\n213\t\n214\tTo test whether the oracle gap is closable, we train a logistic regression verifier on the 5 QE signals using 5-fold cross-validation on SciERC (Table~\\ref{tab:verifier}).\n215\t\n216\t\\begin{table}[t]\n217\t\\centering\n218\t\\small\n219\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc cc}\n220\t\\toprule\n221\tMethod & NER F1 & $\\Delta$greedy & RE F1 & $\\Delta$greedy \\\\\n222\t\\midrule\n223\tGreedy ($T$=0) & 0.644 & --- & 0.404 & --- \\\\\n224\tBest Unsup.\\ (LP) & 0.650 & +0.6pp & 0.410 & +0.6pp \\\\\n225\tTrained Verifier & 0.692 & +4.8pp & 0.442 & +3.8pp \\\\\n226\tOracle & 0.776 & +13.2pp & 0.585 & +18.1pp \\\\\n227\t\\bottomrule\n228\t\\end{tabular}\n229\t\\caption{Trained verifier (LR on 5 signals, 5-fold CV) vs.\\ unsupervised selection on SciERC. The verifier closes 36.4\\% of the NER oracle gap and 21.0\\% of the RE gap, far exceeding unsupervised methods.}\n230\t\\label{tab:verifier}\n231\t\\end{table}\n232\t\n233\tThe trained verifier achieves NER F1 of 0.692 (+4.8pp over greedy), closing 36.4\\% of the 13.2pp oracle gap (Table~\\ref{tab:gap_closure}). For RE, gap closure is 21.0\\% (+3.8pp). Both substantially exceed the best unsupervised signal (LP: +0.6pp for both tasks). Cross-validation standard deviations are small (NER: $\\pm$0.018, RE: $\\pm$0.024), indicating stable performance.\n234\t\n235\t\\begin{table}[t]\n236\t\\centering\n237\t\\small\n238\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n239\t\\toprule\n240\tTask & Oracle Gap & Verifier Gain & Closure \\% & Remaining \\\\\n241\t\\midrule\n242\tNER & 13.2pp & 4.8pp & 36.4 & 63.6 \\\\\n243\tRE & 18.1pp & 3.8pp & 21.0 & 79.0 \\\\\n244\t\\bottomrule\n245\t\\end{tabular}\n246\t\\caption{Oracle gap closure by the trained verifier. NER gap closure (36.4\\%) confirms signals carry sufficient information; the bottleneck is unsupervised aggregation.}\n247\t\\label{tab:gap_closure}\n248\t\\end{table}\n249\t\n250\t\\paragraph{Feature Importance.} Table~\\ref{tab:features} shows normalized logistic regression coefficients. EM and VC dominate for both tasks, consistent with their superior QE metrics. LP and FK contribute minimally, confirming that structural signals carry the majority of discriminative information.\n251\t\n252\t\\begin{table}[t]\n253\t\\centering\n254\t\\small\n255\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n256\t\\toprule\n257\tFeature & NER weight & RE weight \\\\\n258\t\\midrule\n259\tEM & 0.34 & 0.38 \\\\\n260\tVC & 0.28 & 0.26 \\\\\n261\tSJ & 0.21 & 0.15 \\\\\n262\tLP & 0.11 & 0.14 \\\\\n263\tFK & 0.06 & 0.07 \\\\\n264\t\\bottomrule\n265\t\\end{tabular}\n266\t\\caption{Normalized LR feature weights. EM and VC together account for 62--64\\% of model weight across tasks.}\n267\t\\label{tab:features}\n268\t\\end{table}\n269\t\n270\t\\paragraph{Implications.} The verifier result establishes that: (a)~QE signals carry sufficient information to discriminate sample quality---unsupervised heuristics (argmax, majority vote) are the bottleneck, not signal informativeness; (b)~even a simple linear model exploiting signal interactions outperforms any single unsupervised signal by 4.2--3.2pp; (c)~the path forward is trained verifiers, not better unsupervised aggregation rules.\n271\t\n272\tThe lower RE closure (21\\% vs.\\ 36\\%) reflects binary dominance: 43--45\\% of RE instances have zero F1 across all samples (no correct relations extracted), leaving the verifier with no viable candidates. On the non-zero RE subset, closure approaches 35\\%.\n273\t\n274\t\\subsection{Numerical Consistency Audit}\n275\t\\label{sec:audit}\n276\t\n277\tTo ensure soundness, we conduct a systematic cross-section numerical audit verifying all quantitative claims against source experiment data (Table~\\ref{tab:audit}).\n278\t\n279\t\\begin{table}[t]\n280\t\\centering\n281\t\\small\n282\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n283\t\\toprule\n284\tIssue Type & Count & Severity & Status \\\\\n285\t\\midrule\n286\tSelection $\\Delta$ inconsistency & 1 & Medium & Resolved \\\\\n287\tStale $\\rho$ from 512-token run & 1 & Medium & Updated \\\\\n288\tAUROC rounding & 2 & Low & Standardized \\\\\n289\tSet conditioning labels & 2 & Low & Annotated \\\\\n290\tSeed specification & 1 & Low & Added \\\\\n291\t\\bottomrule\n292\t\\end{tabular}\n293\t\\caption{Numerical consistency audit results. All identified issues resolved; no critical (direction-changing) contradictions found. Selection delta unified to +0.2pp macro F1 ($p$=0.29).}\n294\t\\label{tab:audit}\n295\t\\end{table}\n296\t\n297\tThree medium-severity issues were identified and resolved: the selection improvement delta was unified to +0.2pp (macro F1, $p$=0.29, $d$=0.038) across all sections; a stale voting confidence $\\rho$ from a 512-token preliminary run was updated to the 1024-token value (0.379); and AUROC values were standardized to 3 decimal places. No critical issues (directional contradictions) were found, confirming that all claims are traceable to source experiments.\n298\t\n299\t%============================================================================="}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XwiP9E8yHrwSW4dSMLkQ4B","session_id":"6d14e849-04d8-4a95-8f72-941f894ab1f4","uuid":"3f271be8-6f1c-4c0c-834e-6cc35e756792","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:04:18.143Z"}
