{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HU5Fm3ubVnXaXc4c6UCzKk","type":"tool_result","content":"299\t\n300\t%=============================================================================\n301\t\\section{Discussion}\n302\t%=============================================================================\n303\t\n304\t\\paragraph{The QE--Selection Decoupling.}\n305\tOur central finding is that quality \\emph{estimation} and quality \\emph{selection} are governed by different factors for structured IE. QE succeeds because it operates at the population level: across instances, higher-consistency outputs tend to have higher F1. Selection fails because it operates within instances: among $N$ samples from the same input, the consistency signal lacks granularity to identify the best candidate. This decoupling has practical implications---QE-based selective prediction (abstaining on low-confidence instances) remains valuable even when selection provides no marginal gain.\n306\t\n307\t\\paragraph{Capability--Degeneracy Tradeoff.}\n308\tWe observe a qualitative relationship between model capability and instance degeneracy: CoNLL (greedy F1=0.91) shows 55--58\\% degeneracy, SciERC (F1=0.66) shows 21\\%, and WNUT (F1=0.48) shows near-zero. As models improve on a task, output convergence increases, eliminating the diversity that selection requires. This suggests that unsupervised test-time scaling has diminishing returns precisely where models are already strong---a structural inevitability rather than an engineering failure.\n309\t\n310\t\\paragraph{When Would Consistency Help?}\n311\tOur negative selection result applies to fine-tuned models with constrained decoding on established IE benchmarks. Consistency-based selection may still benefit: (a)~in-context learning without fine-tuning (worse calibration), (b)~closed-source APIs where logprob is unavailable, (c)~tasks where models are systematically overconfident, or (d)~compound extraction pipelines where error propagation increases diversity.\n312\t\n313\t\\paragraph{Path Forward.}\n314\tThe trained verifier experiment (\\S\\ref{sec:verifier}) demonstrates that the oracle gap \\emph{is} closable. Even logistic regression on 5 signals achieves 36\\% NER gap closure. This motivates development of lightweight learned verifiers (e.g., DeBERTa-based scorers, reward models) as a practical alternative to unsupervised selection for structured IE. The remaining 64\\% gap likely requires access to richer representations beyond our 5 scalar signals.\n315\t\n316\t%=============================================================================\n317\t\\section{Limitations}\n318\t%=============================================================================\n319\t\n320\t\\begin{itemize}\n321\t    \\item Dataset coverage is limited to English scientific (SciERC), news (CoNLL-2003), and social media (WNUT-17) domains. Informal text, multilingual settings, and domain-specific IE (biomedical, legal) are not evaluated.\n322\t    \\item Model scale is capped at 8B parameters. Larger models (70B+) may exhibit different degeneracy--capability dynamics but are computationally infeasible for our multi-sample protocol.\n323\t    \\item The trained verifier uses oracle F1 labels for supervision, which requires annotated data. Transfer across tasks/domains is not tested.\n324\t    \\item The capability--degeneracy tradeoff is demonstrated across 3 dataset difficulty levels; the relationship is qualitative (monotone) but not formally characterized.\n325\t\\end{itemize}\n326\t\n327\t%=============================================================================\n328\t\\section{Conclusion}\n329\t%=============================================================================\n330\t\n331\tWe present a dual contribution for structured IE test-time scaling: a QE framework demonstrating that confidence signals reliably predict output quality (AUROC 0.82, $\\rho$ 0.44), and a diagnostic analysis revealing why these signals fail at selection. The multi-causal failure---instance degeneracy, logprob compression, and insufficient discriminability---persists across 3 model architectures, 2 tasks, and 3 datasets. A trained verifier closing 36\\% of the oracle gap confirms that the information exists in the signals; the bottleneck is unsupervised aggregation. Our findings redirect the path for structured IE scaling from better unsupervised signals toward lightweight trained verifiers.\n332\t\n333\t%=============================================================================\n334\t% References\n335\t%=============================================================================\n336\t\\begin{thebibliography}{30}\n337\t\n338\t\\bibitem[Wang et al.(2023)]{wang2023selfconsistency}\n339\tXuezhi Wang, Jason Wei, Dale Schuurmans, Quoc Le, Ed Chi, Sharan Narang, Aakanksha Chowdhery, and Denny Zhou. 2023.\n340\t\\newblock Self-consistency improves chain of thought reasoning in language models.\n341\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}.\n342\t\n343\t\\bibitem[Brown et al.(2024)]{brown2024large}\n344\tBradley Brown, Jordan Juravsky, Ryan Ehrlich, et al. 2024.\n345\t\\newblock Large language monkeys: Scaling inference compute with repeated sampling.\n346\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2407.21787}.\n347\t\n348\t\\bibitem[Snell et al.(2024)]{snell2024scaling}\n349\tCharlie Snell, Jaehoon Lee, Kelvin Xu, and Aviral Kumar. 2024.\n350\t\\newblock Scaling LLM test-time compute optimally can be more effective than scaling model parameters.\n351\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2408.03314}.\n352\t\n353\t\\bibitem[Kuhn et al.(2023)]{kuhn2023semantic}\n354\tLorenz Kuhn, Yarin Gal, and Sebastian Farquhar. 2023.\n355\t\\newblock Semantic uncertainty: Linguistic invariances for uncertainty estimation in natural language generation.\n356\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}.\n357\t\n358\t\\bibitem[Manakul et al.(2023)]{manakul2023selfcheckgpt}\n359\tPotsawee Manakul, Adian Liusie, and Mark Gales. 2023.\n360\t\\newblock SelfCheckGPT: Zero-resource black-box hallucination detection for generative large language models.\n361\t\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP}.\n362\t\n363\t\\bibitem[Eikema and Aziz(2022)]{eikema2022}\n364\tBryan Eikema and Wilker Aziz. 2022.\n365\t\\newblock Sampling-based approximations to minimum Bayes risk decoding for neural machine translation.\n366\t\\newblock In \\emph{EACL}.\n367\t\n368\t\\bibitem[Cobbe et al.(2021)]{cobbe2021}\n369\tKarl Cobbe, Vineet Kosaraju, Mohammad Bavarian, Mark Chen, Heewoo Jun, Lukasz Kaiser, Matthias Plappert, Jerry Tworek, Jacob Hilton, Reiichiro Nakano, Christopher Hesse, and John Schulman. 2021.\n370\t\\newblock Training verifiers to solve math word problems.\n371\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2110.14168}.\n372\t\n373\t\\bibitem[Lightman et al.(2023)]{lightman2023}\n374\tHunter Lightman, Vineet Kosaraju, Yura Burda, Harri Edwards, Bowen Baker, Teddy Lee, Jan Leike, and John Schulman. 2023.\n375\t\\newblock Let's verify step by step.\n376\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}.\n377\t\n378\t\\bibitem[Geifman and El-Yaniv(2017)]{geifman2017}\n379\tYonatan Geifman and Ran El-Yaniv. 2017.\n380\t\\newblock Selective classification for deep neural networks.\n381\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}.\n382\t\n383\t\\bibitem[Fisch et al.(2021)]{fisch2021}\n384\tAdam Fisch, Tal Schuster, Tommi Jaakkola, and Regina Barzilay. 2021.\n385\t\\newblock Few-shot conformal prediction with auxiliary tasks.\n386\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}.\n387\t\n388\t\\bibitem[Angelopoulos et al.(2021)]{angelopoulos2021}\n389\tAnastasios Angelopoulos, Stephen Bates, Jitendra Malik, and Michael Jordan. 2021.\n390\t\\newblock Uncertainty sets for image classifiers using conformal prediction.\n391\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}.\n392\t\n393\t\\bibitem[Shelmanov et al.(2021)]{shelmanov2021}\n394\tArtem Shelmanov, Evgenii Tsymbalov, Dmitri Puzyrev, Kirill Fedyanin, Alexander Panchenko, and Maxim Panov. 2021.\n395\t\\newblock How certain is your transformer?\n396\t\\newblock In \\emph{EACL}.\n397\t\n398\t\\bibitem[Lakshminarayanan et al.(2017)]{lakshminarayanan2017}\n399\tBalaji Lakshminarayanan, Alexander Pritzel, and Charles Blundell. 2017.\n400\t\\newblock Simple and scalable predictive uncertainty estimation using deep ensembles.\n401\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}.\n402\t\n403\t\\bibitem[Kuleshov and Liang(2015)]{kuleshov2015}\n404\tVolodymyr Kuleshov and Percy Liang. 2015.\n405\t\\newblock Calibrated structured prediction.\n406\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}.\n407\t\n408\t\\bibitem[N\\'{e}dellec and Xia(2026)]{nedellec2026}\n409\tClaire N\\'{e}dellec and Ruoyu Xia. 2026.\n410\t\\newblock Consistency--accuracy correlation in hard-prompted LLMs for entity and relation extraction.\n411\t\\newblock \\emph{Genomics Informatics}.\n412\t\n413\t\\bibitem[CISC(2025)]{cisc2025}\n414\tConfidence-weighted majority vote for reasoning.\n415\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACL Findings}, 2025.\n416\t\n417\t\\bibitem[Self-Certainty(2026)]{selfcertainty2026}\n418\tSelf-certainty best-of-N via KL-divergence.\n419\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2026. arXiv:2502.18581.\n420\t\n421\t\\bibitem[ReASC(2026)]{reasc2026}\n422\tReliability-aware adaptive self-consistency.\n423\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2601.02970}, 2026.\n424\t\n425\t\\bibitem[Atomic SC(2024)]{atomicsc2024}\n426\tAtomic self-consistency for long-form text.\n427\t\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP}, 2024.\n428\t\n429\t\\bibitem[UQ Survey(2025)]{uqsurvey2025}\n430\tUncertainty quantification for LLMs: A comprehensive survey.\n431\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACL Findings}, 2025.\n432\t\n433\t\\bibitem[Calibration Survey(2025)]{calibsurvey2025}\n434\tUncertainty quantification and calibration for LLMs: A survey.\n435\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2503.15850}, 2025.\n436\t\n437\t\\bibitem[CoCoA(2025)]{cocoa2025}\n438\tArtem Vashurin et al. 2025.\n439\t\\newblock CoCoA: Combining confidence and consistency in MBR-style uncertainty quantification.\n440\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}. arXiv:2502.04964.\n441\t\n442\t\\bibitem[Luan et al.(2018)]{luan2018multi}\n443\tYi Luan, Luheng He, Mari Ostendorf, and Hannaneh Hajishirzi. 2018.\n444\t\\newblock Multi-task identification of entities, relations, and coreference for scientific knowledge graph construction.\n445\t\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP}.\n446\t\n447\t\\bibitem[Tjong Kim Sang and De Meulder(2003)]{tjong2003introduction}\n448\tErik Tjong Kim Sang and Fien De Meulder. 2003.\n449\t\\newblock Introduction to the CoNLL-2003 shared task: Language-independent named entity recognition.\n450\t\\newblock In \\emph{CoNLL}.\n451\t\n452\t\\end{thebibliography}\n453\t\n454\t\\end{document}\n455\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XwiP9E8yHrwSW4dSMLkQ4B","session_id":"6d14e849-04d8-4a95-8f72-941f894ab1f4","uuid":"57bd52e8-b4a5-4f22-adcc-91285523dd6f","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:04:24.050Z"}
