{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Ta3SrxrTMDPNXc5F7Rnmjz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JqmgVhaKvEvNCLs9xhHDeP","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1 cross-response to R2","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1), focused on Methods and Experiments. You have already written your independent review. Now you are reading Reviewer 2's (R2) review focused on Novelty and Related Work. \n\n## Your Original Review Summary (R1)\n- Soundness: 3.0, Excitement: 3.5, Overall: 3.0\n- Key strengths: QE-selection decoupling is novel and well-executed; LoRA rank ablation is elegant; honest statistical reporting\n- Key weaknesses: Small sample sizes for central negative claim; \"model-agnostic\" overclaimed (two 8B architectures); missing entity-token-only LP baseline; numerical inconsistencies between abstract and tables; single RE dataset; verifier uses oracle labels\n- Assessment: Borderline Main/Findings\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty + Related Work)\nR2 scored: Soundness 3.5, Excitement 2.5, Overall 3.0\n\nKey points from R2:\n1. **QE contribution is limited novelty** — \"confirming and extending\" is more benchmark than conceptual advance\n2. **Instance degeneracy is a rebranding** of known model convergence phenomenon (Snell et al. 2024 already discusses compute-optimal allocation conditioning on difficulty)\n3. **LP compression follows naturally** from autoregressive generation for structured outputs — interesting empirical observation but expected\n4. **LoRA rank=8 \"persistence\" experiment is most novel** finding — demonstrates multi-causality\n5. **QE-Selection decoupling is partially tautological** — 4/5 signals produce one score per instance, so they trivially cannot select. The empirical content is only that LP (the one per-sample signal) also fails.\n6. **Capability-degeneracy correlation on 3 data points** is speculation, not contribution\n7. **Missing related work**: MBR literature (Kumar & Byrne, Freitag et al.), active learning/query-by-committee, conformal prediction, best-of-N with process reward models\n8. **CoCoA contrast is unfair** — paper only tests linear combination, not MBR-style combination\n9. **SJ vs surface matching advantage not empirically demonstrated** — EM actually outperforms SJ on some configs\n10. **Title \"Cannot\" overclaims** — should be \"Do Not\" or \"Fail To\"\n11. **Overall: Findings-level paper**\n\n## Your Task\nRespond to R2's review from your Methods/Experiments perspective. In 300-400 words:\n1. Where do you agree with R2?\n2. Where do you disagree, and why?\n3. Does R2's assessment change any of your scores?\n4. What are the most important points of disagreement to resolve?"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10714,"cache_read_input_tokens":55760,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10714},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6d14e849-04d8-4a95-8f72-941f894ab1f4","uuid":"cd2f043e-0900-4024-9fd2-2a628a86efb4"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a11d4e87b408f6690","tool_use_id":"toolu_01JqmgVhaKvEvNCLs9xhHDeP","description":"R1 cross-response to R2","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1), focused on Methods and Experiments. You have already written your independent review. Now you are reading Reviewer 2's (R2) review focused on Novelty and Related Work. \n\n## Your Original Review Summary (R1)\n- Soundness: 3.0, Excitement: 3.5, Overall: 3.0\n- Key strengths: QE-selection decoupling is novel and well-executed; LoRA rank ablation is elegant; honest statistical reporting\n- Key weaknesses: Small sample sizes for central negative claim; \"model-agnostic\" overclaimed (two 8B architectures); missing entity-token-only LP baseline; numerical inconsistencies between abstract and tables; single RE dataset; verifier uses oracle labels\n- Assessment: Borderline Main/Findings\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty + Related Work)\nR2 scored: Soundness 3.5, Excitement 2.5, Overall 3.0\n\nKey points from R2:\n1. **QE contribution is limited novelty** — \"confirming and extending\" is more benchmark than conceptual advance\n2. **Instance degeneracy is a rebranding** of known model convergence phenomenon (Snell et al. 2024 already discusses compute-optimal allocation conditioning on difficulty)\n3. **LP compression follows naturally** from autoregressive generation for structured outputs — interesting empirical observation but expected\n4. **LoRA rank=8 \"persistence\" experiment is most novel** finding — demonstrates multi-causality\n5. **QE-Selection decoupling is partially tautological** — 4/5 signals produce one score per instance, so they trivially cannot select. The empirical content is only that LP (the one per-sample signal) also fails.\n6. **Capability-degeneracy correlation on 3 data points** is speculation, not contribution\n7. **Missing related work**: MBR literature (Kumar & Byrne, Freitag et al.), active learning/query-by-committee, conformal prediction, best-of-N with process reward models\n8. **CoCoA contrast is unfair** — paper only tests linear combination, not MBR-style combination\n9. **SJ vs surface matching advantage not empirically demonstrated** — EM actually outperforms SJ on some configs\n10. **Title \"Cannot\" overclaims** — should be \"Do Not\" or \"Fail To\"\n11. **Overall: Findings-level paper**\n\n## Your Task\nRespond to R2's review from your Methods/Experiments perspective. In 300-400 words:\n1. Where do you agree with R2?\n2. Where do you disagree, and why?\n3. Does R2's assessment change any of your scores?\n4. What are the most important points of disagreement to resolve?","uuid":"604b8e69-359c-4fe3-9e09-29e9345bb135","session_id":"6d14e849-04d8-4a95-8f72-941f894ab1f4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1), focused on Methods and Experiments. You have already written your independent review. Now you are reading Reviewer 2's (R2) review focused on Novelty and Related Work. \n\n## Your Original Review Summary (R1)\n- Soundness: 3.0, Excitement: 3.5, Overall: 3.0\n- Key strengths: QE-selection decoupling is novel and well-executed; LoRA rank ablation is elegant; honest statistical reporting\n- Key weaknesses: Small sample sizes for central negative claim; \"model-agnostic\" overclaimed (two 8B architectures); missing entity-token-only LP baseline; numerical inconsistencies between abstract and tables; single RE dataset; verifier uses oracle labels\n- Assessment: Borderline Main/Findings\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty + Related Work)\nR2 scored: Soundness 3.5, Excitement 2.5, Overall 3.0\n\nKey points from R2:\n1. **QE contribution is limited novelty** — \"confirming and extending\" is more benchmark than conceptual advance\n2. **Instance degeneracy is a rebranding** of known model convergence phenomenon (Snell et al. 2024 already discusses compute-optimal allocation conditioning on difficulty)\n3. **LP compression follows naturally** from autoregressive generation for structured outputs — interesting empirical observation but expected\n4. **LoRA rank=8 \"persistence\" experiment is most novel** finding — demonstrates multi-causality\n5. **QE-Selection decoupling is partially tautological** — 4/5 signals produce one score per instance, so they trivially cannot select. The empirical content is only that LP (the one per-sample signal) also fails.\n6. **Capability-degeneracy correlation on 3 data points** is speculation, not contribution\n7. **Missing related work**: MBR literature (Kumar & Byrne, Freitag et al.), active learning/query-by-committee, conformal prediction, best-of-N with process reward models\n8. **CoCoA contrast is unfair** — paper only tests linear combination, not MBR-style combination\n9. **SJ vs surface matching advantage not empirically demonstrated** — EM actually outperforms SJ on some configs\n10. **Title \"Cannot\" overclaims** — should be \"Do Not\" or \"Fail To\"\n11. **Overall: Findings-level paper**\n\n## Your Task\nRespond to R2's review from your Methods/Experiments perspective. In 300-400 words:\n1. Where do you agree with R2?\n2. Where do you disagree, and why?\n3. Does R2's assessment change any of your scores?\n4. What are the most important points of disagreement to resolve?"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01JqmgVhaKvEvNCLs9xhHDeP","session_id":"6d14e849-04d8-4a95-8f72-941f894ab1f4","uuid":"d7229a2f-5c36-4da9-8333-b5a01a28f6ff","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:06:33.270Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Ta3SrxrTMDPNXc5F7Rnmjz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CCrn4Sk72k8WHUtgH4Jh3o","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2 cross-response to R1","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2), focused on Novelty and Related Work. You have already written your independent review. Now you are reading Reviewer 1's (R1) review focused on Methods and Experiments.\n\n## Your Original Review Summary (R2)\n- Soundness: 3.5, Excitement: 2.5, Overall: 3.0\n- Key strengths: Honest framing, thorough ablations, structured IE angle provides modest novelty\n- Key weaknesses: Core insight partially tautological (4/5 signals can't rank by construction); \"contributing factors\" rebrand known phenomena; no constructive path forward; title overclaims \"Cannot\"\n- Assessment: Findings-level\n\n## R1's Review (Methods + Experiments)\nR1 scored: Soundness 3.0, Excitement 3.5, Overall 3.0\n\nKey points from R1:\n1. **Signal design has gaps**: Semantic entropy not evaluated; entity-token-only LP not tested (despite paper's own hypothesis about LP dilution); no normalized LP variants\n2. **Aggregate vs per-sample handling is confused**: Paper's Equation (1) defines SJ as per-instance aggregate, but Section 3.3 says SJ produces per-sample scores. Tension not resolved — if SJ does rank candidates, the \"4/5 signals cannot rank\" claim is misleading.\n3. **Statistical reporting is proper** (bootstrap CI, p=0.39, d=0.038) but only one significance test for the central claim\n4. **Small sample sizes**: SciERC NER n=529, RE n=397; effective RE size ~220 after removing zero-F1. Bootstrap CI width 7.2pp. Could be power issue, not fundamental impossibility.\n5. **Cross-model validation insufficient**: Two 8B architectures + one 4B from same family ≠ \"model-agnostic\"\n6. **LoRA rank ablation is the strongest experiment** — elegant causal design\n7. **Verifier uses oracle labels** — limits practical utility; only LR tested, no other classifiers\n8. **Multiple numerical inconsistencies**: Abstract AUROC 0.82 vs table max 0.749 (SciERC) / 0.816 (CoNLL); rho 0.44 vs max ~0.436; verifier gap closure numbers disagree between paper sections\n9. **No code/data release mentioned**\n10. **Reproducibility gaps**: LoRA hyperparameters, vLLM config, Hungarian matching thresholds not specified\n\n## Your Task\nRespond to R1's review from your Novelty/Related Work perspective. In 300-400 words:\n1. Where do you agree with R1?\n2. Where do you disagree, and why?\n3. Does R1's assessment change any of your scores?\n4. What are the most important points of disagreement to resolve?"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10714,"cache_read_input_tokens":55760,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10714},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6d14e849-04d8-4a95-8f72-941f894ab1f4","uuid":"bfc443f2-3a9d-42b0-b617-9d86172783d0"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a0b1246e0adbb7b79","tool_use_id":"toolu_01CCrn4Sk72k8WHUtgH4Jh3o","description":"R2 cross-response to R1","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2), focused on Novelty and Related Work. You have already written your independent review. Now you are reading Reviewer 1's (R1) review focused on Methods and Experiments.\n\n## Your Original Review Summary (R2)\n- Soundness: 3.5, Excitement: 2.5, Overall: 3.0\n- Key strengths: Honest framing, thorough ablations, structured IE angle provides modest novelty\n- Key weaknesses: Core insight partially tautological (4/5 signals can't rank by construction); \"contributing factors\" rebrand known phenomena; no constructive path forward; title overclaims \"Cannot\"\n- Assessment: Findings-level\n\n## R1's Review (Methods + Experiments)\nR1 scored: Soundness 3.0, Excitement 3.5, Overall 3.0\n\nKey points from R1:\n1. **Signal design has gaps**: Semantic entropy not evaluated; entity-token-only LP not tested (despite paper's own hypothesis about LP dilution); no normalized LP variants\n2. **Aggregate vs per-sample handling is confused**: Paper's Equation (1) defines SJ as per-instance aggregate, but Section 3.3 says SJ produces per-sample scores. Tension not resolved — if SJ does rank candidates, the \"4/5 signals cannot rank\" claim is misleading.\n3. **Statistical reporting is proper** (bootstrap CI, p=0.39, d=0.038) but only one significance test for the central claim\n4. **Small sample sizes**: SciERC NER n=529, RE n=397; effective RE size ~220 after removing zero-F1. Bootstrap CI width 7.2pp. Could be power issue, not fundamental impossibility.\n5. **Cross-model validation insufficient**: Two 8B architectures + one 4B from same family ≠ \"model-agnostic\"\n6. **LoRA rank ablation is the strongest experiment** — elegant causal design\n7. **Verifier uses oracle labels** — limits practical utility; only LR tested, no other classifiers\n8. **Multiple numerical inconsistencies**: Abstract AUROC 0.82 vs table max 0.749 (SciERC) / 0.816 (CoNLL); rho 0.44 vs max ~0.436; verifier gap closure numbers disagree between paper sections\n9. **No code/data release mentioned**\n10. **Reproducibility gaps**: LoRA hyperparameters, vLLM config, Hungarian matching thresholds not specified\n\n## Your Task\nRespond to R1's review from your Novelty/Related Work perspective. In 300-400 words:\n1. Where do you agree with R1?\n2. Where do you disagree, and why?\n3. Does R1's assessment change any of your scores?\n4. What are the most important points of disagreement to resolve?","uuid":"15e64c87-e0fb-48b7-8dc7-ec2887f33938","session_id":"6d14e849-04d8-4a95-8f72-941f894ab1f4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2), focused on Novelty and Related Work. You have already written your independent review. Now you are reading Reviewer 1's (R1) review focused on Methods and Experiments.\n\n## Your Original Review Summary (R2)\n- Soundness: 3.5, Excitement: 2.5, Overall: 3.0\n- Key strengths: Honest framing, thorough ablations, structured IE angle provides modest novelty\n- Key weaknesses: Core insight partially tautological (4/5 signals can't rank by construction); \"contributing factors\" rebrand known phenomena; no constructive path forward; title overclaims \"Cannot\"\n- Assessment: Findings-level\n\n## R1's Review (Methods + Experiments)\nR1 scored: Soundness 3.0, Excitement 3.5, Overall 3.0\n\nKey points from R1:\n1. **Signal design has gaps**: Semantic entropy not evaluated; entity-token-only LP not tested (despite paper's own hypothesis about LP dilution); no normalized LP variants\n2. **Aggregate vs per-sample handling is confused**: Paper's Equation (1) defines SJ as per-instance aggregate, but Section 3.3 says SJ produces per-sample scores. Tension not resolved — if SJ does rank candidates, the \"4/5 signals cannot rank\" claim is misleading.\n3. **Statistical reporting is proper** (bootstrap CI, p=0.39, d=0.038) but only one significance test for the central claim\n4. **Small sample sizes**: SciERC NER n=529, RE n=397; effective RE size ~220 after removing zero-F1. Bootstrap CI width 7.2pp. Could be power issue, not fundamental impossibility.\n5. **Cross-model validation insufficient**: Two 8B architectures + one 4B from same family ≠ \"model-agnostic\"\n6. **LoRA rank ablation is the strongest experiment** — elegant causal design\n7. **Verifier uses oracle labels** — limits practical utility; only LR tested, no other classifiers\n8. **Multiple numerical inconsistencies**: Abstract AUROC 0.82 vs table max 0.749 (SciERC) / 0.816 (CoNLL); rho 0.44 vs max ~0.436; verifier gap closure numbers disagree between paper sections\n9. **No code/data release mentioned**\n10. **Reproducibility gaps**: LoRA hyperparameters, vLLM config, Hungarian matching thresholds not specified\n\n## Your Task\nRespond to R1's review from your Novelty/Related Work perspective. In 300-400 words:\n1. Where do you agree with R1?\n2. Where do you disagree, and why?\n3. Does R1's assessment change any of your scores?\n4. What are the most important points of disagreement to resolve?"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CCrn4Sk72k8WHUtgH4Jh3o","session_id":"6d14e849-04d8-4a95-8f72-941f894ab1f4","uuid":"1d42cb5c-eb10-4105-8e83-a8bb77d1121e","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:06:47.846Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a11d4e87b408f6690","tool_use_id":"toolu_01JqmgVhaKvEvNCLs9xhHDeP","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1 cross-response to R2","usage":{"total_tokens":14727,"tool_uses":0,"duration_ms":18167},"uuid":"b81b0f92-3513-4b5f-91ee-2ae9995a5fa6","session_id":"6d14e849-04d8-4a95-8f72-941f894ab1f4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JqmgVhaKvEvNCLs9xhHDeP","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## R1 Response to R2's Review\n\n**Points of Agreement**\n\nI agree with R2 on several fronts. The \"Cannot\" vs \"Do Not\" title distinction (point 10) is valid and aligns with my own concern about overclaiming from limited sample sizes. The 3-datapoint capability-degeneracy correlation (point 6) is indeed speculation — I flagged the same statistical insufficiency. R2's observation that 4/5 QE signals trivially cannot select (point 5) is sharp and partially undermines the paper's framing of QE-selection decoupling as a broader finding. The missing MBR literature (point 7) is a legitimate gap I overlooked.\n\n**Points of Disagreement**\n\nI disagree that instance degeneracy is merely \"rebranding\" (point 2). Snell et al. discuss compute-optimal allocation abstractly; this paper provides concrete empirical characterization showing degeneracy emerges at specific consistency thresholds and correlates with extraction difficulty in a task-specific way. The mechanism is documented, not just named. Whether that constitutes novelty vs. thorough empirical work is debatable, but it is not a simple relabeling.\n\nI also push back on dismissing the LoRA rank experiment as \"only\" showing multi-causality (point 4). From a methods perspective, showing that a single hyperparameter change shifts the dominant failure mode between extraction errors and format errors — while leaving overall consistency unchanged — is a genuinely useful diagnostic finding. R2 acknowledges it is the most novel result but underweights its methodological utility.\n\nOn the CoCoA comparison (point 8), R2 is correct that only linear combination was tested, but the paper's point is narrower: that CoCoA's scoring mechanism does not address selection when underlying candidates are degenerate. Testing MBR-style combination would strengthen the paper but does not invalidate the existing comparison.\n\nRegarding SJ vs EM (point 9), I partially agree — the advantage is inconsistent. However, SJ enables the structural analysis that EM cannot, which is the paper's actual contribution. Performance parity is sufficient justification.\n\n**Score Revision**\n\nR2's point 5 (partial tautology of QE-selection decoupling) modestly weakens my excitement. I would revise Excitement from 3.5 to 3.0, keeping Soundness at 3.0 and Overall at 3.0. This reinforces my Findings-level assessment.\n\n**Key Disagreements to Resolve**\n\nThe critical question is whether \"thorough empirical characterization of a negative result\" constitutes a Findings or Main contribution. R2 frames it as confirmation; I see it as structured diagnosis. The distinction determines the venue recommendation."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a11d4e87b408f6690 (use SendMessage with to: 'a11d4e87b408f6690' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 15349\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 18169</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6d14e849-04d8-4a95-8f72-941f894ab1f4","uuid":"f64b9a32-20ac-48c2-a21b-c6b90fa63856","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:06:51.440Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1), focused on Methods and Experiments. You have already written your independent review. Now you are reading Reviewer 2's (R2) review focused on Novelty and Related Work. \n\n## Your Original Review Summary (R1)\n- Soundness: 3.0, Excitement: 3.5, Overall: 3.0\n- Key strengths: QE-selection decoupling is novel and well-executed; LoRA rank ablation is elegant; honest statistical reporting\n- Key weaknesses: Small sample sizes for central negative claim; \"model-agnostic\" overclaimed (two 8B architectures); missing entity-token-only LP baseline; numerical inconsistencies between abstract and tables; single RE dataset; verifier uses oracle labels\n- Assessment: Borderline Main/Findings\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty + Related Work)\nR2 scored: Soundness 3.5, Excitement 2.5, Overall 3.0\n\nKey points from R2:\n1. **QE contribution is limited novelty** — \"confirming and extending\" is more benchmark than conceptual advance\n2. **Instance degeneracy is a rebranding** of known model convergence phenomenon (Snell et al. 2024 already discusses compute-optimal allocation conditioning on difficulty)\n3. **LP compression follows naturally** from autoregressive generation for structured outputs — interesting empirical observation but expected\n4. **LoRA rank=8 \"persistence\" experiment is most novel** finding — demonstrates multi-causality\n5. **QE-Selection decoupling is partially tautological** — 4/5 signals produce one score per instance, so they trivially cannot select. The empirical content is only that LP (the one per-sample signal) also fails.\n6. **Capability-degeneracy correlation on 3 data points** is speculation, not contribution\n7. **Missing related work**: MBR literature (Kumar & Byrne, Freitag et al.), active learning/query-by-committee, conformal prediction, best-of-N with process reward models\n8. **CoCoA contrast is unfair** — paper only tests linear combination, not MBR-style combination\n9. **SJ vs surface matching advantage not empirically demonstrated** — EM actually outperforms SJ on some configs\n10. **Title \"Cannot\" overclaims** — should be \"Do Not\" or \"Fail To\"\n11. **Overall: Findings-level paper**\n\n## Your Task\nRespond to R2's review from your Methods/Experiments perspective. In 300-400 words:\n1. Where do you agree with R2?\n2. Where do you disagree, and why?\n3. Does R2's assessment change any of your scores?\n4. What are the most important points of disagreement to resolve?","agentId":"a11d4e87b408f6690","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## R1 Response to R2's Review\n\n**Points of Agreement**\n\nI agree with R2 on several fronts. The \"Cannot\" vs \"Do Not\" title distinction (point 10) is valid and aligns with my own concern about overclaiming from limited sample sizes. The 3-datapoint capability-degeneracy correlation (point 6) is indeed speculation — I flagged the same statistical insufficiency. R2's observation that 4/5 QE signals trivially cannot select (point 5) is sharp and partially undermines the paper's framing of QE-selection decoupling as a broader finding. The missing MBR literature (point 7) is a legitimate gap I overlooked.\n\n**Points of Disagreement**\n\nI disagree that instance degeneracy is merely \"rebranding\" (point 2). Snell et al. discuss compute-optimal allocation abstractly; this paper provides concrete empirical characterization showing degeneracy emerges at specific consistency thresholds and correlates with extraction difficulty in a task-specific way. The mechanism is documented, not just named. Whether that constitutes novelty vs. thorough empirical work is debatable, but it is not a simple relabeling.\n\nI also push back on dismissing the LoRA rank experiment as \"only\" showing multi-causality (point 4). From a methods perspective, showing that a single hyperparameter change shifts the dominant failure mode between extraction errors and format errors — while leaving overall consistency unchanged — is a genuinely useful diagnostic finding. R2 acknowledges it is the most novel result but underweights its methodological utility.\n\nOn the CoCoA comparison (point 8), R2 is correct that only linear combination was tested, but the paper's point is narrower: that CoCoA's scoring mechanism does not address selection when underlying candidates are degenerate. Testing MBR-style combination would strengthen the paper but does not invalidate the existing comparison.\n\nRegarding SJ vs EM (point 9), I partially agree — the advantage is inconsistent. However, SJ enables the structural analysis that EM cannot, which is the paper's actual contribution. Performance parity is sufficient justification.\n\n**Score Revision**\n\nR2's point 5 (partial tautology of QE-selection decoupling) modestly weakens my excitement. I would revise Excitement from 3.5 to 3.0, keeping Soundness at 3.0 and Overall at 3.0. This reinforces my Findings-level assessment.\n\n**Key Disagreements to Resolve**\n\nThe critical question is whether \"thorough empirical characterization of a negative result\" constitutes a Findings or Main contribution. R2 frames it as confirmation; I see it as structured diagnosis. The distinction determines the venue recommendation."}],"totalDurationMs":18169,"totalTokens":15349,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6472,"cache_read_input_tokens":8251,"output_tokens":623,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6472},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":623,"cache_read_input_tokens":8251,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6472,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6472,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a0b1246e0adbb7b79","tool_use_id":"toolu_01CCrn4Sk72k8WHUtgH4Jh3o","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R2 cross-response to R1","usage":{"total_tokens":14757,"tool_uses":0,"duration_ms":19605},"uuid":"df1dc3f8-d195-4e9a-ae85-3cbcb5ee6ae0","session_id":"6d14e849-04d8-4a95-8f72-941f894ab1f4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CCrn4Sk72k8WHUtgH4Jh3o","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## R2 Response to R1's Review\n\n**Points of Agreement**\n\nI agree strongly with R1's points 2 and 8. The tension between Equation (1)'s per-instance aggregate and Section 3.3's per-sample ranking is not merely a methodological issue — it undermines the paper's central novelty claim. If SJ does produce per-sample discriminative scores, then the \"4/5 signals cannot rank\" framing, which I flagged as partially tautological, is worse than tautological: it is misleading. R1's numerical inconsistencies (Abstract AUROC 0.82 vs table max 0.749/0.816) further erode confidence in the paper's headline claims, reinforcing my concern about the title overclaiming \"Cannot.\"\n\nI also agree that the cross-model validation (point 5) is insufficient. From the related work angle, prior self-consistency literature (Wang et al. 2023, Chen et al. 2024) already showed signal behavior varies across model families. Testing within Qwen variants tells us little about generality.\n\n**Points of Disagreement**\n\nR1 scores Excitement at 3.5, which I find generous. R1's own analysis reveals that the signal design omits semantic entropy — the most theoretically motivated uncertainty signal for structured generation — and that the entity-token LP hypothesis (the paper's most interesting mechanistic conjecture) is never actually tested. These are not merely \"gaps\"; they mean the paper's negative results may not hold under the most promising signal variants. A negative-result paper that leaves the strongest baselines untested has limited novelty value. The related work on token-level probability for IE (Ye & Durrett 2022, Kuhn et al. 2023) already suggests that naive sequence LP is insufficient. The paper does not adequately position against these.\n\nI partially disagree with R1's generous assessment of the LoRA ablation (point 6). While the causal design is elegant, as a novelty contribution it merely confirms what calibration literature predicts: finetuning sharpens output distributions. This is known from Kadavath et al. (2022) and Tian et al. (2023).\n\n**Score Adjustments**\n\nR1's findings on numerical inconsistencies and the SJ definitional confusion strengthen my concern about overclaiming. I lower my Soundness from 3.5 to 3.0 to align with R1. My Excitement remains at 2.5 — R1's analysis, if anything, reinforces my view that the novelty is limited. Overall stays at 3.0.\n\n**Key Disagreements to Resolve**\n\nThe critical question for discussion: does the SJ definitional confusion (R1 point 2) constitute a factual error that requires revision, or a presentation issue? If SJ genuinely ranks candidates, the paper's core claim collapses from \"fundamental impossibility\" to \"most signals fail but one works partially.\" This distinction determines whether the paper is findings-level or below threshold."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a0b1246e0adbb7b79 (use SendMessage with to: 'a0b1246e0adbb7b79' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 15440\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 19607</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6d14e849-04d8-4a95-8f72-941f894ab1f4","uuid":"bd4c7cfc-5bd8-4862-8db9-bd168187aacb","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:07:07.455Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2), focused on Novelty and Related Work. You have already written your independent review. Now you are reading Reviewer 1's (R1) review focused on Methods and Experiments.\n\n## Your Original Review Summary (R2)\n- Soundness: 3.5, Excitement: 2.5, Overall: 3.0\n- Key strengths: Honest framing, thorough ablations, structured IE angle provides modest novelty\n- Key weaknesses: Core insight partially tautological (4/5 signals can't rank by construction); \"contributing factors\" rebrand known phenomena; no constructive path forward; title overclaims \"Cannot\"\n- Assessment: Findings-level\n\n## R1's Review (Methods + Experiments)\nR1 scored: Soundness 3.0, Excitement 3.5, Overall 3.0\n\nKey points from R1:\n1. **Signal design has gaps**: Semantic entropy not evaluated; entity-token-only LP not tested (despite paper's own hypothesis about LP dilution); no normalized LP variants\n2. **Aggregate vs per-sample handling is confused**: Paper's Equation (1) defines SJ as per-instance aggregate, but Section 3.3 says SJ produces per-sample scores. Tension not resolved — if SJ does rank candidates, the \"4/5 signals cannot rank\" claim is misleading.\n3. **Statistical reporting is proper** (bootstrap CI, p=0.39, d=0.038) but only one significance test for the central claim\n4. **Small sample sizes**: SciERC NER n=529, RE n=397; effective RE size ~220 after removing zero-F1. Bootstrap CI width 7.2pp. Could be power issue, not fundamental impossibility.\n5. **Cross-model validation insufficient**: Two 8B architectures + one 4B from same family ≠ \"model-agnostic\"\n6. **LoRA rank ablation is the strongest experiment** — elegant causal design\n7. **Verifier uses oracle labels** — limits practical utility; only LR tested, no other classifiers\n8. **Multiple numerical inconsistencies**: Abstract AUROC 0.82 vs table max 0.749 (SciERC) / 0.816 (CoNLL); rho 0.44 vs max ~0.436; verifier gap closure numbers disagree between paper sections\n9. **No code/data release mentioned**\n10. **Reproducibility gaps**: LoRA hyperparameters, vLLM config, Hungarian matching thresholds not specified\n\n## Your Task\nRespond to R1's review from your Novelty/Related Work perspective. In 300-400 words:\n1. Where do you agree with R1?\n2. Where do you disagree, and why?\n3. Does R1's assessment change any of your scores?\n4. What are the most important points of disagreement to resolve?","agentId":"a0b1246e0adbb7b79","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## R2 Response to R1's Review\n\n**Points of Agreement**\n\nI agree strongly with R1's points 2 and 8. The tension between Equation (1)'s per-instance aggregate and Section 3.3's per-sample ranking is not merely a methodological issue — it undermines the paper's central novelty claim. If SJ does produce per-sample discriminative scores, then the \"4/5 signals cannot rank\" framing, which I flagged as partially tautological, is worse than tautological: it is misleading. R1's numerical inconsistencies (Abstract AUROC 0.82 vs table max 0.749/0.816) further erode confidence in the paper's headline claims, reinforcing my concern about the title overclaiming \"Cannot.\"\n\nI also agree that the cross-model validation (point 5) is insufficient. From the related work angle, prior self-consistency literature (Wang et al. 2023, Chen et al. 2024) already showed signal behavior varies across model families. Testing within Qwen variants tells us little about generality.\n\n**Points of Disagreement**\n\nR1 scores Excitement at 3.5, which I find generous. R1's own analysis reveals that the signal design omits semantic entropy — the most theoretically motivated uncertainty signal for structured generation — and that the entity-token LP hypothesis (the paper's most interesting mechanistic conjecture) is never actually tested. These are not merely \"gaps\"; they mean the paper's negative results may not hold under the most promising signal variants. A negative-result paper that leaves the strongest baselines untested has limited novelty value. The related work on token-level probability for IE (Ye & Durrett 2022, Kuhn et al. 2023) already suggests that naive sequence LP is insufficient. The paper does not adequately position against these.\n\nI partially disagree with R1's generous assessment of the LoRA ablation (point 6). While the causal design is elegant, as a novelty contribution it merely confirms what calibration literature predicts: finetuning sharpens output distributions. This is known from Kadavath et al. (2022) and Tian et al. (2023).\n\n**Score Adjustments**\n\nR1's findings on numerical inconsistencies and the SJ definitional confusion strengthen my concern about overclaiming. I lower my Soundness from 3.5 to 3.0 to align with R1. My Excitement remains at 2.5 — R1's analysis, if anything, reinforces my view that the novelty is limited. Overall stays at 3.0.\n\n**Key Disagreements to Resolve**\n\nThe critical question for discussion: does the SJ definitional confusion (R1 point 2) constitute a factual error that requires revision, or a presentation issue? If SJ genuinely ranks candidates, the paper's core claim collapses from \"fundamental impossibility\" to \"most signals fail but one works partially.\" This distinction determines whether the paper is findings-level or below threshold."}],"totalDurationMs":19607,"totalTokens":15440,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6502,"cache_read_input_tokens":8251,"output_tokens":684,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6502},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":684,"cache_read_input_tokens":8251,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6502,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6502,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}}}
